# C#

Formatting is CSharpier's (`.cursor/rules/engineering.mdc`). This rule covers what a formatter cannot.

## Style

- A property with a one-line getter and a setter of several lines does not pass the gate: CSharpier puts the getter on one line and SA1504 refuses accessors of mixed shapes. The setter is one line too, and calls a method that does the work (`WindowFrames.TextRendering`, PR #313). I1c met it again (`Win32Window.TouchpadViewport`), as this line was not read first; a read-only property and a method that replaces the value (`UseTouchpadViewport`) avoid it (PR #410).
- A function pointer cast whose type does not fit on one line does not pass the gate either: CSharpier breaks the type over several lines and SA1003 refuses the operator at the line's end. Give the `[UnmanagedCallersOnly]` function short parameter types (`void*` for a pointer it does not read, `int` for an enum) and convert inside, as `ViewportEventHandler` does (PR #410).

- Modern C# is allowed: `var` when the type is apparent on the right, collection expressions, target-typed `new`, primary constructors, file-scoped namespaces, `required` members, `ref struct`, `scoped`, `allows ref struct`.
- Private fields are `_camelCase`, static ones included. Constants are PascalCase. No `this.` unless needed to disambiguate. Interfaces start with `I`.
- Namespaces follow assembly names (`XylemUI` for `XylemUI.Core`'s public types, `XylemUI.Text`, `XylemUI.Platform.Windows`), not folders.
- One type per file by default. A file name matches its main type.
- No `using` alias to shorten a BCL type name. Qualify the name where it collides.
- Library `await` uses `ConfigureAwait(false)` except where the code must resume on the UI thread, which says so in a comment. `CancellationToken` is the last parameter.
- A new overload goes above the XML docs and attributes of the member it joins, not between them and that member. In L1b an overload put between `[MethodImpl(MethodImplOptions.AggressiveInlining)]` and `UsesPercent` took the attribute from it.
- No alignment component in an interpolated string (`{value, 4}`). CSharpier writes `{value, 4}` and StyleCop then reports SA1001, so the format check and the build cannot both pass; pad with `PadLeft` instead (S8b).

## Types

Make an invalid state unrepresentable; do not document it away or catch it at run time.

- One choice, one slot: an enum, a sealed record hierarchy, or a generic parameter, not a `bool` plus a nullable that the `bool` makes meaningless.
- A parameter that belongs to one variant lives in that variant (`LayoutAlgorithm.Flex(FlexProps)`).
- Handles are distinct types (`Handle<T>`), so a `NodeHandle` cannot be passed where a `ScopeHandle` is expected.
- The `default` of a handle or id type never refers to a live value, so a zeroed field means "none" and cannot alias the first item. Start generations or ids at 1, and test `default` against the first allocated value. In S2 the arena started generations at 0, and `default(Handle<T>)` was alive while slot 0 was in use; a review found it, not a test. Stored links use that `default`, not `Nullable<Handle<T>>`, which adds a flag and padding (design §2.5).

```csharp
// BAD — the first slot is Index 0, Generation 0, which is also default(Handle<T>)
return new Handle<T>(index, slot.Generation);   // Generation starts at 0

// GOOD — a new slot starts at generation 1, so default is never alive (Handle<T>.IsNone)
_slots[index].Generation = 1;
```
- A struct that `UiNode` holds (`LayoutState`) cannot have a field of `Handle<UiNode>`: the runtime does not load `UiNode` (`TypeLoadException`). Such a link is a field of `UiNode` itself, beside `Parent` and `FirstChild` (`UiNode.BaselineChild`, L1b).
- A value whose `null` means "the OS's" (or a default that can change) is kept as `null` and resolved where it is read, not where it is stored: a value resolved when stored stays old after the OS's changes. P02b2's text field wrote Windows' selection colors into the leaf when it set the selection, so a change of Windows' colors did not reach a selection until it moved (PR #500's review).
- A value whose `null` takes a default says the one value `null` takes, for every combination with the other values that can stand in for it. PR #515's `ImeAttributeRun.LineColor` said "null is the text's color", and left open whether that is `TextColor` when one is given or the field's color (the review found it).
- A method that cannot fail returns its value, not a `bool`/`out` pair or a nullable.
- A public method does not throw because the caller used the wrong phase of the same type; split the type instead.
- Core types are `internal` (design §2.9.1). The public surface is `Signal`, `Element`, the component functions, the interfaces Platform and Rendering implement, and the snapshot and message types they receive.

## Errors

- Argument and state checks throw through `ThrowHelper` (`.cursor/rules/perf.mdc`), with one-sentence English messages that name the condition.
- An internal invariant violation calls `Invariant.Fail` (debug: throws `InvariantViolationException`, so a test can observe it; release: `Environment.FailFast`, design §0.1). Never `Debug.Fail`: without a debugger attached it ends the process. It is not caught.
- Public exception types are few and documented in the XML docs of the members that throw them.
- A constructor that changes process-wide state (a static, such as `ScopeTracker.IsEnabled`) puts it back, and frees what it made, when a later step throws. `HeadlessApp`'s constructor turned the tracker on and then ran the root function. A root function that threw left the tracker on for the rest of the process, and later leak checks counted the failed app's scopes (the review of PR #461).
- A `catch` that runs cleanup which can throw keeps the exception it caught: when the cleanup throws too, throw an `AggregateException` with the caught exception first, then the cleanup's. In S9 `Scope.Create` disposed the scope of a body that threw, and a cleanup's exception replaced the body's (a review found it). The rule holds in benchmarks and tools too: in M1a the E2E watch of the screen ended the launched app with `Process.Kill` in a `catch` without it (a review found it again).
- A comment that states how a runtime or OS API behaves names what was checked: the call, the version, and the result. In M1a a comment said `Process.Kill` does nothing for an exited process before anyone had run it; the check found it does not throw on .NET 10.0.13, and its documentation names a `Win32Exception` for a process that is exiting.
- A call that can fail by its result is checked: `Matrix3x2.Invert` returns `false` for a singular matrix and fills the result with NaN. A singular transform is a normal input (a scale of 0 starts an entering animation). In R4d a clip in such a transform carried NaN into the bounds of a layer until the review found it (PR #268).
- Work counted across threads counts the threads that fail to start: a `Thread.Start` that throws leaves its share undone, and a wait for the count never returns. Do that share on the calling thread (R4d's stated shaders, PR #268).
- One quantity computed in two places and compared (a line's width from running sums and from a sum along the line) is computed the same way in both, or compared with a tolerance whose size and reason are written beside it (T1b's `ShapingResult.Tolerance`, PR #293). The tolerance goes into every comparison of that quantity, a value that came back through a layout with a padding added and taken away included; and of two stages in a row, the later accepts twice the tolerance, so that their bounds do not meet. V1a's text leaf compared a width with the text's widest line without it, and a padded leaf at its own max-content width wrapped; then Pass1 and Pass2 took the same bound. Reviews found both (PR #301).
- A check that runs in Debug builds only does not change the state it checks. V1a's check of a text leaf's content widths measured through the leaf's cache, added an entry for a min-content width the layout never asked for, and made the leaf's memory differ between Debug and Release; it measures into an entry of its own now (PR #301).
- A static value that threads share and that is made when first read uses `Lazy<T>` with `LazyThreadSafetyMode.ExecutionAndPublication`, not `??=`. With `??=`, two threads reading `FontFallback.Bundled` together each opened a bundled font, each with an id of its own; it showed when V1a's text leaves read it on the UI thread beside the font fallback tests (Issue #303).
- A check on a combination of a positional record's members runs where the value is taken (a setter, a constructor's parameter), not in the record. `with` copies the value and sets members through `init`, and never calls the primary constructor, so a check there does not see `value with { IsAliased = true }`; a check in `init` sees one member set before the other and refuses a valid result. `WindowFrames` refuses bilevel text rendering with ClearType coverage in its constructor and its `TextRendering` setter (PR #317). The first answer to the review gave a different reason, which was wrong.
- A `global using` alias exists only inside its assembly. A public signature that uses it shows the real type to apps (`ScopeHandle` is `Handle<ScopeData>` outside Core, S9); decide the public name when the API is public.

## XML docs

- Public and internal members under `src/` have `summary`, `param`, and `returns`, and `exception` when a caller might be unsure; private members may. StyleCop enforces it (`documentInternalElements`, row Q6, Issue #449): internal types are an API between the assemblies that see them through `InternalsVisibleTo`, and their readers are agents and maintainers (D3). Sentences end with a period. Properties start with "Gets" / "Gets or sets"; constructors with "Initializes a new instance of the <see cref="T"/> class".
- XML docs state the caller's contract only. How it works lives in `docs/design.ja.md`; refer to the section instead of restating it.
- Use `<inheritdoc />` when an implementation has the interface's contract.
- A member that returns one shared instance of a public `IDisposable` says in its XML docs that callers do not dispose it (`FontFallback.Bundled`, PR #152 review).
- Wording and terminology: `.agents/skills/english-writing/SKILL.md`.
